  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Pholiota flammans, commonly known as the flaming scalycap, is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by its distinctive scaly, flame-like appearance on the cap. This wood-decaying fungus belongs to the Strophariaceae family and is found in temperate regions including New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Scaly Cap",
      "description": "Bright yellow to orange cap covered with reddish or rust-colored scales arranged in a radiating pattern, resembling flames",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ring on Stem",
      "description": "Distinctive ring (annulus) on the upper portion of the yellowish stem, which may disappear with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Color",
      "description": "Yellow gills that darken to olive-brown or rusty-brown as the mushroom matures",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Pholiota flammans grows on decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and wood chips in moist forests throughout New England, typically appearing in spring through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'flammans' refers to the mushroom's flaming appearance, which resembles small fires on the forest floor and makes it one of the most visually striking wood-decay fungi in the region.",
